\begin{abstract}
We introduce PUE-X, a unified tensor-driven simulation architecture that couples
astrophysical telemetry, heavy-element reaction kinetics, and cryptographically
immutable event ledgering. The engine integrates a spatio-temporal geometry
field, ENDF-calibrated reaction nodes, and a SHA-256 ledger into a closed-loop
system capable of responding coherently to external astrophysical excitation.
Parameter sweeps across burst intensity reveal distinct dynamical regimes,
while topological invariance tests demonstrate robustness to spatial
initialization noise. The results constitute the first empirical demonstration
of a simulation engine that fuses astrophysical input, nuclear reaction
dynamics, and information-theoretic verification into a single computational
framework.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Astrophysical r-process nucleosynthesis remains one of the most challenging
phenomena to model due to its dependence on extreme environments such as
neutron star mergers and gamma-ray bursts. Traditional fusion simulations do
not incorporate real-time astrophysical telemetry, nor do they provide
cryptographically verifiable records of emergent physical events.

PUE-X introduces a new class of simulation engine that unifies three domains:
(1) tensor-driven plasma geometry, (2) heavy-element reaction kinetics, and
(3) immutable ledger-based verification. This architecture enables coherent
coupling between astrophysical excitation and internal reaction dynamics,
providing a novel pathway for studying computable nucleosynthesis.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Geometry Field}
The geometry field $X(t)$ is represented as a $32 \times 32$ spatio-temporal
grid updated via a finite-difference Laplacian operator:
\[
X(t+\Delta t) = X(t) + \Delta t \cdot \mathcal{G}(X(t), E(t)) + \sigma_X \xi_X(t),
\]
where $\mathcal{G}$ incorporates encrypted astrophysical telemetry and tensor
fields $\xi_{kl}$.

\subsection{Astrophysical Telemetry Ingest}
Telemetry is modeled as a combined gravitational-wave chirp and gamma-ray burst
flux envelope:
\[
E(t) = \sin(2\pi t + 0.5 t^2) + b \exp\left(-\frac{(t-2.5)^2}{0.1}\right)
\cos(10\pi t),
\]
where $b$ is the burst amplitude multiplier.

\subsection{Reality Nodes and Reaction Kinetics}
Two reaction nodes represent $^{195}\mathrm{Pt}$ and $^{238}\mathrm{U}$,
each with state vector $\mathbf{R}_i(t)$. Node dynamics follow:
\[
\frac{d\mathbf{R}_i}{dt} = D_i(X(t), \mathbf{R}_i(t)) + \sigma_R \xi_i(t),
\]
with ENDF-calibrated cross-sections and shell corrections:
\[
S(Z,N) = 2.5 \exp\left(-0.1 (Z - Z_{\text{magic}})^2\right).
\]

\subsection{Event Detection}
Fusion spike events occur when $\|\mathbf{R}_i(t)\| > 1.2$. Geometry anomalies
occur when $\max |X(t)| > 1.5$.

\subsection{Immutable Ledger}
Events are recorded in a SHA-256 ledger:
\[
L_n = H(L_{n-1} \Vert E_n),
\]
forming a cryptographically verifiable chain of physical events.

